1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:19,000
Same words. Different verdicts. The first claim had no test run behind it —
PEX checked the workspace, not the narration.

2
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:25,000
This worker added a pytest.ini pointing the suite at an empty dir —
PEX sealed the acceptance surface before it started.

3
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:35,000
Semantic rulings run NVIDIA Nemotron-3-Super on Nebius Token Factory.
The trajectories are recorded runs — labeled, never faked.

4
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:50,000
The worker's turn ended before the human answered — the ruling still
landed on the durable intent ledger, marked session_terminal.
Human intent outlives the worker.

5
00:00:50,500 --> 00:01:02,000
Told to edit a test the goal forbids, the worker called PEX's own MCP
server and escalated itself with three authored options.

6
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:14,000
A ledger that only blocks would be a trap — the same sealed goal signs
off a compliant run.

7
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:20,000
Every adjudication exports a hash-chained evidence pack —
verifiable offline, no trust in us required.

8
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:30,000
And it catches real workers on real runs — this OpenCode session
claimed done on a decoy checker.

9
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:38,500
Not a mock — a real Nemotron completion on Token Factory,
catalog discovery to 200 OK, receipt committed.

10
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,500
Deterministic where correctness demands it,
Nemotron where judgment is needed.
